{
  "title": "From calcium release to relaxation",
  "sources": [
    "CH43S53",
    "CH43S54",
    "CH43S55",
    "CH43S56"
  ],
  "stages": [
    {
      "start": 0,
      "title": "Excitation and coupling",
      "text": "Muscle contraction begins with electrical excitation. The supplied sequence reaches voltage-sensitive receptors in the transverse tubules and opens ryanodine channels. Released calcium enables actin to bind myosin."
    },
    {
      "start": 13.72,
      "title": "Filament sliding",
      "text": "Now watch thin filaments move inward relative to thick filaments. Their overlap increases and the sarcomere shortens. The motion represents cross-bridge activity, not shortening of the individual filaments. The packet identifies a continuing requirement for ATP and phosphocreatine."
    },
    {
      "start": 31.14,
      "title": "Relaxation",
      "text": "During relaxation, calcium returns to the sarcoplasmic reticulum, cross-bridges detach, and the sarcomere lengthens. Follow the reverse motion. This film uses a textbook figure with schematic motion to separate excitation, coupling, contraction, and relaxation."
    }
  ],
